CLAIMS=[
('VOO, IVV and SPY track the S&P 500; SPY was the first, in 1993; together they hold more than $2 trillion.','three exchange-traded funds that track the S&P 500',R+'SPY launch (January 1993) and combined assets above $2 trillion in 2026 are consistent with analyst recall; fund AUM not fetched. '+U,[]),
('Bessembinder: about 26,000 US stocks since 1926; 57.4% failed to beat a Treasury bill over their lifetime; about 4% produced the entire net gain; 86 companies made half.','A finance\nprofessor named Hendrik Bessembinder',P+'BESS abstract (SSRN 2900447, inspected via archived copy): "Four out of every seven common stocks" since 1926 have lifetime buy-and-hold returns below one-month Treasuries; "the best-performing four percent" explain the net gain, while other stocks collectively matched T-bills. 4/7 = 57.1%, consistent with 57.4%. '+R+'The 25,967-stock count, exact 57.4% and 86-firm half are from the paper body and were not reinspected. Verified in substance.',['BESS']),
('More than 9,000 of 26,000 were delisted; the median delisted stock lost about 92%.','more than\n9,000 were delisted',R+'Not in the inspected abstract; not reverified. '+U,[]),
('Jack Bogle launched the first retail index fund in 1976, sought $150 million and raised $11.3 million (about 7%); underwriters suggested cancelling; "Bogle\'s folly"; an Uncle Sam poster called index funds un-American.','He wanted to raise $150\nmillion',R+'The $150M target, $11.3M raised, "Bogle\'s Folly" and the "Index funds are un-American" poster are well documented in Vanguard and Bogle histories, but the Vanguard page could not be fetched this session (DNS failure). '+C+'11.3/150 = 7.5%, so "roughly 7%" is slightly low.',[]),
('In that era an 8% sales load was normal.','normal to hand a salesman 8%',R+'Front-end loads of ~8.5% (the FINRA cap) were common in the 1970s. Consistent; not fetched.',[]),
('Vanguard is mutually owned: funds own the company and investors own the funds.','the funds own the company and you own the\nfunds',R+'Accurate description of Vanguard\'s structure; not fetched.',[]),
('SPY traded about a million shares on day one; within months volume fell to about 18,000 shares a day; a 9-ft inflatable spider hung on the AMEX floor.','traded about a million shares on day\none',R+'The opening-day volume (~1.1M) and inflatable spider appear in SPY histories; the 18,000-share figure is not reverified. '+U,[]),
('The chip designer (Nvidia) fell more than 50% four times, once close to 90%, and took four years to recover; the streaming company fell 76% in 7 months; the online bookstore fell 94%.','four\nseparate occasions where it lost more\nthan half its value',R+'Amazon\'s ~94% 1999-2001 drawdown and Nvidia\'s ~90% 2000-2002 drawdown are consistent with recall. Netflix fell ~76-80% in 2011 over about four months, not seven. Exact recovery periods not reverified. '+C+'$100,000 x (1 - 0.94) = $6,000 reproduces.',[]),
('Morningstar\'s "Mind the Gap" finds investors trail their funds by about one percentage point a year; some academics argue the gap is smaller.','Morningstar tracks the gap',R+'Morningstar\'s recent Mind the Gap studies put the gap at about 1.1-1.2 points a year; the caveat about academic critique is fair. Not fetched.',[]),
('Two researchers studied tens of thousands of brokerage accounts; the households that traded most earned about 11% a year while the market returned close to 18%.','The households that traded\nmost earned around 11% a year',P+'BO2000 abstract (Journal of Finance, April 2000, PDF inspected): 66,465 households, 1991-1996; most active traders earned 11.4% a year vs a 17.9% market return. Verified. Note the 1991-1996 sample period is old.',['BO2000']),
('Buffett\'s 2007 $1 million bet: the index fund was up about 126% after 10 years; Ted Seides\' five funds-of-funds averaged roughly 2% a year.','the index fund\nwas up about 126%',P+'BRK2017 (Berkshire 2017 shareholder letter, archived PDF inspected): S&P 500 index fund final gain 125.8% (8.5% a year); funds-of-funds A-E final gains 21.7%, 42.3%, 87.7%, 2.8%, 27.0%, average annual 2.0%, 3.6%, 6.5%, 0.3%, 2.4%. '+C+'The simple mean of the five annual rates is 2.96%, so "roughly 2%" understates them. The 126% is verified. The bet ran 2008-2017; the counterparty was Protégé Partners (Seides).',['BRK2017']),
('Buffett instructed that money left to his wife go almost entirely into an S&P 500 index fund.','money\nleft to his wife',R+'Buffett\'s 2013 letter (90% S&P 500 index fund, 10% short-term Treasuries) is consistent with recall; not refetched.',[]),
('Fidelity: about 645,000 401(k) millionaires; average balance about $141,000; median about $32,800 (more than four times smaller).','The average balance is about\n$141,000',P+'FIDQ1 (Fidelity Q1 2026 retirement analysis, page inspected): average 401(k) balance $141,000 as of 31 March 2026. Verified. The page text read did not show the millionaire count or a median, so 645,000 and $32,800 remain unverified. '+C+'141,000 / 32,800 = 4.30x, consistent with "more than four times". A later Q2 2026 page shows a $155,800 average (data to 30 June 2026); whether it was public before the 14 August 2026 upload is unknown.',['FIDQ1']),
('Vanguard: average about $168,000, median about $44,000; roughly two-thirds of participants are in a professionally managed allocation, mostly a single target-date fund.','Average around 168,000',R+'Consistent with Vanguard How America Saves 2025/2026 ranges by analyst recall; not fetched. '+U,[]),
('Two of the three funds charge 0.03% ($30 a year on $100,000); the average equity mutual fund charged over 1% in the 1990s.','Two of these three funds charge\nyou 3/100 of 1%',R+'VOO and IVV 0.03%, SPY ~0.0945%; ICI asset-weighted equity fund expense ratios were ~1% in the 1990s. Consistent; not fetched. '+C+'0.0003 x 100,000 = $30.',[]),
('In a recent year about 7% of ETFs distributed capital gains vs over half of mutual funds; VOO has never distributed a capital gain.','around\n7% of exchange-traded funds',R+'Not fetched; the direction is accurate. The VOO claim is consistent with Vanguard disclosures by recall. '+U,[]),
('The top 10 companies are about 40% of the S&P 500, the highest concentration in over half a century.','The 10 largest companies now make up\nroughly 40%',R+'Top-10 weight near 38-40% in 2025-2026 is consistent with recall; not fetched.',[]),
('The Big Three are the largest shareholder in about 88% of S&P 500 companies.','something like 88% of the companies',R+'Fichtner, Heemskerk and Garcia-Bernardo (2017) reported 88% using 2015 data. Accurate but dated; not refetched.',[]),
('Vanguard\'s model expects US stocks to return about 4-6% a year over the next decade.','somewhere around 4 to 6% a year',R+'Vanguard VCMM US equity projections in 2025-2026 were near 3.5-6%. Broadly consistent; not fetched. The forecast is a projection, not a fact.',[]),
('Japan peaked in 1989 and took 34 years to recover; US stocks went nowhere for 13 years after 2000.','The Japanese market peaked in\n1989',R+'Nikkei\'s December 1989 peak was regained in February 2024 (~34 years). The US flat period depends on the index and dividends. Consistent.',[]),
('Bennett lands about four years of work behind Tessa ($600/month vs ~$900 with match, 25 years).','He is behind by roughly the value of\nfour more years',C+'The hypothetical is not specified enough to reproduce. For reference, $600 vs $900/month for 25 years at 7% gives $486,043 vs $729,065 (the match alone is 50%). The "four years" result is unverifiable.',[]),
]
TITLE='Authentic title "The 3 ETFs That Created More Millionaires Than Any Stock" uses a numbered list ("3 ETFs") with a superlative comparison claim. The script names VOO, IVV and SPY as one S&P 500 exposure, then builds the case through Bessembinder skew, Bogle and SPY history, Buffett\'s bet and 401(k) data. The "created more millionaires" superlative is not measured anywhere (no count of millionaires by holding). TYPICAL against the cohort. Association only.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White background. Left: the recurring cartoon man in a grey T-shirt grinning and pointing right. Right: a heavy black headline "BUY THIS ETF NOW!" over a rising bar chart with blue "ETF 1", red "ETF 2" and yellow "ETF 3" bars, a green trend arrow, a money bag and cash stacks, with a curved black arrow pointing at the money. At 320x180 the headline, pointing character and three coloured bars stay legible; the bar labels become small. The singular "THIS ETF" conflicts with the title\'s "3 ETFs", and its buy-now urgency conflicts with the script\'s patience thesis and closing disclaimer.'
SCRIPT='A narrative essay with composite characters Bennett (stock picker) and Tessa (default index investor): seller\'s-remorse hook; channel pitch; names VOO/IVV/SPY; Bessembinder skew; index logic; Bogle 1976 and Vanguard structure; SPY creation; drawdowns of big winners; Bennett\'s 4 a.m. sale; $600/month simulation with an employer match; behaviour gap (Morningstar, Barber-Odean); Buffett\'s bet; Fidelity/Vanguard mean vs median; target-date defaults; fees and ETF tax efficiency; community plug; counter-arguments (concentration, Big Three ownership, lower forecasts, long droughts, concentration builds fortunes); interrupted compounding; the "four years" payoff; disclaimer; reflective close. Directly verified: Bessembinder 4-of-7 and 4%; Barber-Odean 11.4% vs 17.9%; Buffett bet 125.8%; Fidelity Q1 2026 average $141,000. Inaccurate: funds-of-funds averaged ~3% (2.0-6.5% each), not "roughly 2%"; Netflix\'s 2011 fall took ~4 months, not 7. The 645,000 millionaires, $32,800 median and several historical details are unverified. Balanced: includes explicit counter-arguments and a mean-vs-median correction. The stored ending is complete.'
BEATS=['Hook: selling something you were right about; Bennett vs Tessa.','Channel pitch; the three S&P 500 ETFs.','Objection: "just buy the index".','Bessembinder: 57.4%, 4%, 86 firms, delistings.','Index as arithmetic: own all winners.','Bogle 1976, Bogle\'s folly, loads, Vanguard structure.','Nate Most and SPY; spider; volume collapse.','Drawdowns of Nvidia, Netflix, Amazon.','Bennett sells at 4 a.m.','$600/month simulation; Tessa\'s match.','Behaviour gap: Morningstar; Barber-Odean.','Buffett\'s bet.','Fidelity/Vanguard averages vs medians.','Target-date defaults.','Fees and ETF tax efficiency; community plug.','Counter-arguments: concentration, Big Three, forecasts, droughts.','Concentration builds, diversification keeps; interrupted compounding.','Four-year penalty; blame on system design.','Disclaimer; close and comment prompt.']
